  "preview": "A hotel charges a 12% resort fee and 9% lodging tax — both on the base nightly rate. Maya's final total is $169.40. What was the base rate before the fee and tax?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Compose and decompose percent increases applied to a common base, recognize when percentages add vs. compound, and solve for an unknown base from a final total using an equation rather than guess-and-check.",
    "studentPrompt": "Maya is booking a hotel room for one night near the state science fair. The hotel's posted rules:\n\n- The hotel adds a 12% resort fee, calculated on the base nightly rate.\n- The state then adds a 9% lodging tax, also calculated on the base nightly rate (not on the resort fee).\n\nMaya's total charge for the night is $169.40.\n\n1. What was the base nightly rate before the resort fee and tax were added?\n2. Show how to check your answer by working forward from the base rate to $169.40.\n3. A second hotel chain charges a flat 21% \"convenience surcharge\" on the base rate (no separate fee, no separate tax). If the second hotel's final total for one night is also $169.40, is its base rate the same as Maya's, higher, or lower? Justify without doing a full new calculation.\n4. A third hotel applies a 12% resort fee on the base rate, then charges 9% lodging tax on the (base + resort fee) — tax stacks on top of the fee. With the same $169.40 total, would the base rate here be higher or lower than Maya's? Explain why before computing.",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A simple hotel facade with three lit windows, a front door, and a small receipt next to it showing line items 'Base / Resort Fee / Tax / Total' without numbers.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#eef3f7\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"185\" width=\"480\" height=\"35\" fill=\"#cfd8df\"/><g><rect x=\"40\" y=\"45\" width=\"200\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#bcae8e\" stroke=\"#5e553e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"40\" y=\"45\" width=\"200\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#5e553e\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#fbf7ef\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">HOTEL</text><g fill=\"#fff3a6\" stroke=\"#7a6418\" stroke-width=\"1\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"78\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/><rect x=\"104\" y=\"78\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"78\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/><rect x=\"196\" y=\"78\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/></g><g fill=\"#e8eef7\" stroke=\"#7a8a9a\" stroke-width=\"1\"><rect x=\"58\" y=\"115\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/><rect x=\"104\" y=\"115\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/><rect x=\"196\" y=\"115\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\"/></g><rect x=\"150\" y=\"115\" width=\"28\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#5a3a14\" stroke=\"#2a1a08\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"172\" cy=\"152\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#e8a33d\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(290,55)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"150\" height=\"130\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M0,130 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8 l10,-8 l10,8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2a2a2a\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">RECEIPT</text><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#3a3a3a\"><text x=\"18\" y=\"50\">Base rate</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"end\">$ __</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"70\">Resort fee 12%</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"end\">$ __</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"90\">Lodging tax 9%</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"end\">$ __</text></g><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1a1a1a\" font-weight=\"700\"><text x=\"18\" y=\"118\">TOTAL</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"end\">$169.40</text></g></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the receipt with the blanks. Read the two hotel rules out loud. Ask: 'Maya paid $169.40 — what was the room actually priced at?' Don't preview the equation move. Send groups to the boards and watch who guesses-and-checks vs. who writes 1.21x = 169.40.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: calculators (release after Q1 estimates are committed)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "percent",
      "percent-of-a-quantity",
      "reverse-percent",
      "equations",
      "compound-vs-additive-percents"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC task. The base-rate problem invites guess-and-check (low floor) but rewards groups that recognize 'add 12% and 9% of the same base' as 121% of base. Q3 and Q4 are the diamond — they push at the difference between adding percentages and compounding them, which is exactly where 7th graders' intuition breaks.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Strong groups write base + 0.12 * base + 0.09 * base = 169.40, simplify to 1.21 * base = 169.40, solve base = $140. Other groups guess-and-check ($140 -> 16.80 + 12.60 + 140 = 169.40, check). A few try to peel off 9% and then 12% separately, often incorrectly.\n- Q2: $140 base + $16.80 (12% of 140) + $12.60 (9% of 140) = $169.40.\n- Q3: 21% surcharge on the base produces the SAME equation 1.21 * base = 169.40, so the base rate is identical ($140). The 12% + 9% on the same base is equivalent to a flat 21%.\n- Q4: If tax stacks on (base + fee), the multiplier is base * 1.12 * 1.09 = base * 1.2208. Since the multiplier is LARGER than 1.21, the same $169.40 total comes from a SMALLER base rate ($169.40 / 1.2208 ≈ $138.76).",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Computing 12% of 169.40 to 'undo' the resort fee — treating $169.40 as the base instead of the total.\n- Subtracting 12% then subtracting 9% sequentially from $169.40 ($169.40 * 0.88 * 0.91) — this both undoes the wrong direction and assumes compounding.\n- Believing 'add 12% then add 9% on the new amount' is the same as 'add 21%' — it isn't (this is the exact misconception Q4 surfaces).\n- For Q3, jumping to compute without noticing the algebraic equivalence — wasting time re-solving.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Why does 'base + 12% of base + 9% of base' collapse to 'base * 1.21'? Walk it through with a bar model.\n- In Q3, the second hotel charges 21% — and the base rate is exactly the same as Maya's. When does it stop being true that 12% + 9% = 21%?\n- In Q4, tax stacks on the fee. The multiplier ends up 1.2208 instead of 1.21. Where does that extra 0.0008 come from in plain English?",
    "extensions": "- A different state charges a 15% lodging tax on the base rate AND 15% on the resort fee. The 12% resort fee is unchanged. If Maya's total is still $169.40, what is the base rate? Compare with the original.\n- Maya finds a 10% off promo code that applies to the base rate only (before fees and tax are calculated). With the original 12% fee and 9% tax both on the discounted base, her total is $169.40. What was the original (pre-discount) base rate?\n- Design your own hotel pricing policy: pick a single 'all-in' percent surcharge that produces exactly the same final price as Maya's hotel for ANY base rate. Explain why a single percent can always replace 'fee + tax on the same base,' but never replace 'fee, then tax on (base + fee).'",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Let b = base rate. b + 0.12b + 0.09b = 1.21b = 169.40. Base = 169.40 / 1.21 = $140.00.\n2. Check: 12% of 140 = $16.80. 9% of 140 = $12.60. Total = 140 + 16.80 + 12.60 = $169.40.\n3. Same base. The second hotel's 21% on the base rate gives the same equation 1.21 * b = 169.40, so b = $140. Adding 12% and 9% of the same base is algebraically identical to adding 21% of the base.\n4. Lower base. Multiplier becomes 1.12 * 1.09 = 1.2208 (bigger than 1.21), so the same total $169.40 corresponds to a smaller base: 169.40 / 1.2208 ≈ $138.76.",
